{"id": "13cdb6cd-96eb-47ac-8b8d-59ee950cd585", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I notice AWS-DisableS3BucketPublicReadWrite only toggles RestrictPublicBuckets and IgnorePublicAcls, leaving BlockPublicAcls/BlockPublicPolicy untouched — and since PutPublicAccessBlock overwrites the entire config, any unspecified fields default to false, which could actually weaken existing protections. ConfigureS3BucketPublicAccessBlock avoids this gotcha by setting all four fields true by default.

I'll recommend that one, noting it needs an AutomationAssumeRole with s3:PutBucketPublicAccessBlock and s3:GetBucketPublicAccessBlock permissions, and that account-level blocking might also matter. For rollback safety, I should capture the current config via get-public-access-block before making changes — and since I'm uncertain whether the bucket's account matches my current session, I won't actually execute anything, just lay out the plan clearly and concisely.

